"peer_ticker":"INTC",
"peer_disclosure_id":"intc_2024_1A_D016",
"relationship":"same_topic",
"comparison":"Both companies disclose significant uncertainty regarding the pace and market realization of AI product adoption. AMD warns there is 'no assurance about the rate and pace of adoption' of AI capabilities in Client products. Intel mirrors this risk by stating that the markets for AI products are 'difficult to predict' and that its 'significant investments... may be insufficient,' explicitly questioning 'whether demand will materialize' for its AI PC and data center offerings.",
"target_sentence_ids":["amd_2024_1A_P021_S006"],
"peer_sentence_ids":["intc_2024_1A_P036_S001","intc_2024_1A_P036_S003"]
},
{
"peer_ticker":"NVDA",
"peer_disclosure_id":"nvda_2024_1A_D020",
"relationship":"related_topic",
"comparison":"Both companies highlight the difficulty of forecasting demand in emerging AI markets, though they differ in their specific exposure. AMD focuses on the 'Client segment' and the uncertainty of 'AI PC' adoption rates. NVDA focuses on the 'Data Center' segment and the difficulty of estimating demand for 'generative AI models' and new use cases, noting that 'long-term trajectory is unknown.' Both identify AI as a driver of complexity in demand estimation, with AMD citing historical 'replacement cycle changes' and NVDA citing the challenge of matching supply to uncertain future demand.",
"target_sentence_ids":["amd_2024_1A_P021_S006","amd_2024_1A_P021_S007"],
"peer_sentence_ids":["nvda_2024_1A_P022_S003","nvda_2024_1A_P022_S004"]
},
